n" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518096 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186517657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518096 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fb900ce9-eab3-5acd-b944-c83320b552a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518147 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518147 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343666" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518147 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518147 #edb/ref 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+18y=6{sin}^{2}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518147 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5fd4a0bf-2844-5191-8ab1-3fd5afa7e748" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518147 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=18\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\sqrt{2}t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\sqrt{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=6{sin}^{2}⁡pt$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =3\\sqrt{2}.$ Since \n\n$$\nf(t)=6{sin}^{2}⁡pt=3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2pt,\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $2p.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=\\frac{\\omega }{2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518148 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518148 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94240" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518148 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518148 #edb/ref 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518148 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6127495a-5135-5ebc-930f-1823afa46170" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518148 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=3\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{3}t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{3}t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency $p$ of the external periodic force $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt$ coincides with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=\\sqrt{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518240 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186518266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518240 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "373883cf-0b34-53c8-a1b3-d0897fad83b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518240 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186518147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518259 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186518240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518259 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6ea7ee19-1db2-5615-9f9e-e31df6e39383" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518259 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186518148 #edb/ref 17592186045